{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"canthal tilt\" (+ determined caused depends) (+ eyelid orbit orbital anatomy))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_1184422327#4_2642745179","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2538","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_1184422327#4_2642745179\" , \"end_char\": 2865.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Makes a Person’s Eyes Attractive? What Makes a Person’s Eyes Attractive? Attractive Eyes vs. Non-attractive Eyes Attractive Unattractive Eye Orbit Structure Eyelid Exposure High Palpebral Fissure Length (Width of the Eye) Sweeping, Curvy Eyelids (Shape)<cover>Canthal tilt Deep-set Thick, dark eyelashes Spacing (Inter-pupillary Distance) Morphing an Unattractive Eye Area Conclusion Cancel reply \" , \"segment\": \"Hooded eyes”, as they are called, show very little to no exposure of the upper eyelid. Eyelid exposure is generally determined </cover>by the shape and positioning of the upper orbit, along with the fat deposits and soft tissue structure in that area. The latter most likely plays a greater role. Taylor Kinney is an extreme example of a person with hooded eyes. He has absolutely no visible upper eyelid exposure (it’s completely covered by the upper orbital bone and soft tissue). For the most part, this is considered attractive (more so on men) – however, it’s very possible for eyes to be overly ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_854428510#0_1932998045","rank":1,"relevance":1}]}}
