{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_603346943#2_1468435431","relevance":2,"reason":"The full document is dedicated to canthal tilt and gives useful supporting evidence: positive canthal tilt is a 5–8 degree upward angle between the medial and lateral canthi, is associated with youth/feminine appearance, and aging loosens eyelid tendons so the canthus becomes more negative. However, it still does not fully explain why some people naturally have positive tilt beyond sex/youth and tendon laxity.","learned":"Positive canthal tilt depends on the angle between the canthi; aging can make tilt more negative as eyelid tendons loosen, so youthful/tighter eyelid support helps maintain a positive tilt."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"palpebral fissure slant\" \"palpebral fissure inclination\" \"canthal tilt\") (+ \"lateral canthus\" \"medial canthus\") (+ genetics ethnic sex age anatomy))","reason":"Search for more clinical or anthropometric documents that explain natural positive tilt as variation in palpebral fissure slant related to anatomy, genetics, ethnicity, sex, or age."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"palpebral fissure slant\" \"palpebral fissure inclination\" \"canthal tilt\") (+ \"lateral canthus\" \"medial canthus\") (+ genetics ethnic sex age anatomy))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_603346943#1_1468433492","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2543","rank":3,"snippet":"Dropping of the Canthi Canthal Tilt Correction: Canthopexy vs. Canthoplasty Canthal Tilt Surgery and Blepharoplasty \" , \"segment\": \"canthopexy and canthoplasty. Canthal tilt — the angle between the inner and outer corners of the eye — is a key factor in facial aesthetics. The point at which the upper and lower eyelids meet on the outside corner of the eye is known as the lateral canthus, whereas the inner corner of the eye is known as the medial canthus. Collectively, the lateral canthus and <cover>medial canthus are referred to as the canthi. Time tends to have a negative effect on the canthal tilt in both men and women. “ As people age </cover>the tendons of the eyelids get looser and the tilt of the canthus becomes more negative,” says David A. Schlessinger, MD, an ophthalmologist cosmetic surgeon based in Woodbury, New York. People with a negative canthal tilt often ask plastic surgeons about the appearance of their eyes and whether there is anything they can do to improve it. Luckily, there is. “It’s desirable to help move the canthus to a more positive angle in older patients to help restore a more youthful look,” Schlessinger says, “and this ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_1397690825#5_3080888894","rank":1,"relevance":2},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_23_1397690825#6_3080890346","rank":2,"relevance":2}]}}
