{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_603346943#1_1468433492","relevance":2,"reason":"This is another segment of the same canthal-tilt surgery article and gives direct partial evidence: canthal tilt is the angle between medial and lateral canthi, and aging loosens eyelid tendons so tilt becomes more negative. It is useful for age/tendon support but not a full explanation of natural positive tilt.","learned":"Canthal tilt is controlled by the relative positions of the inner and outer eyelid corners; with age, eyelid tendons loosen and the canthus tends to droop toward a more negative tilt."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"upslanting palpebral fissures\" \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\") (+ normal variant ethnic racial familial genetic))","reason":"Search clinical terminology for upward eye slant/positive tilt with normal-variant, familial/genetic, and population terms to find direct explanations of why some people naturally have it."}}
