{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_524764763#1_934422697","relevance":3,"reason":"This clinical reference segment is dedicated to palpebral fissure findings and directly defines upslanting palpebral fissure as the medial canthus appearing lower than the lateral canthus, which matches positive canthal tilt. It also directly lists example causes/associated conditions including Trisomy 21/Down syndrome, Trisomy 9, microcephaly, and Prader-Willi syndrome.","learned":"Positive/upslanting canthal tilt can be explained anatomically by a lower medial canthus than lateral canthus, and clinically it may be associated with genetic/developmental conditions such as Down syndrome, Trisomy 9, microcephaly, and Prader-Willi syndrome."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\" (+ causes associated syndrome genetic chromosomal))","reason":"Search another synonym for upslanting palpebral fissures with cause/association terms to find additional dedicated clinical evidence for why some people have positive/upward canthal tilt."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\" (+ causes associated syndrome genetic chromosomal))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1404150389#10_2212327399","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2571","rank":1,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"Nystagmus may also account for a substantial extent of visual acuity loss. Other common causes of decreased visual acuity in Down syndrome patients include cataracts and high refractive errors. Accommodation Poor accommodative ability has been found in Down syndrome children. Using dynamic retinoscopy, studies have found that 32% to 100% of Down syndrome children have an abnormally low ability to accommodate, measured by accommodative response. Inaccurate accommodation has been found to be associated with hypermetropia and strabismus. Eyelids and Midface The most common findings are <cover>upward slanting palpebral fissures, with a prevalence ranging from 63% to 82% in Down syndrome </cover>children, and large epicanthal folds, ranging from 31% to 97%. Blepharitis is also a common finding in Down syndrome patients. The prevalence of blepharitis in children with Down syndrome ranges from 3% to 34.5%. The wide range in prevalence may be due to differing criteria for diagnosing blepharitis. The high rate of blepharitis has been speculated to be due to impaired immune or due to skin abnormalities in Down syndrome individuals.\" , \"start_char\": 7014.000000 , \"title\": \"Trisomy 21/Down Syndrome - "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"44/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_19_1404150389#10_2212327399","relevance":2,"reason":"This Down syndrome ophthalmology page is not dedicated to positive canthal tilt generally, but it gives direct supporting evidence that upward-slanting palpebral fissures are common in Down syndrome children, with reported prevalence of 63% to 82%.","learned":"Genetic/chromosomal conditions such as Down syndrome can be associated with upward-slanting palpebral fissures, a positive-canthal-tilt-like feature."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"upward slanted palpebral fissures\" \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\") (+ causes cause \"causes list\" diagnosis treatments))","reason":"Search another clinical synonym for upslanted palpebral fissures with explicit causes-list wording to find additional dedicated disease-information pages that directly explain why some people have positive/upward canthal tilt."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"upward slanted palpebral fissures\" \"upward slanting palpebral fissures\") (+ causes cause \"causes list\" diagnosis treatments))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_285670057#3_489218409","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2572","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_16_285670057#3_489218409\" , \"end_char\": 5173.000000 , \"headings\": \"Down Syndrome Down Syndrome Practice Essentials Signs and symptoms of Down syndrome<cover>Diagnosis of Down syndrome Management \" , \"segment\": \"Flat occiput and a flattened facial appearance Small brachycephalic head Epicanthal folds Flat nasal bridge Upward-slanting palpebral fissures</cover>Brushfield spots Small nose and small mouth Protruding tongue Low-set, small, and dysplastic ears Short neck and generous nuchal skin General physical features in patients with Down syndrome may include the following: Shortened extremities Short, broad hands, with short fifth finger with hypoplasia of the middle phalanx and clinodactyly, along with single transverse palmar creases (~60% of patients) Joint hyperextensibility or hyperflexibility Neuromuscular hypotonia Diastasis recti Dry skin Premature aging Congenital heart defects Complications of Down syndrome can involve almost every organ system of the body. See Presentation for more detail. Diagnosis of Down syndrome Laboratory studies that may be helpful include the following: Complete blood count with differential Bone marrow examination to rule out leukemia Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) and thyroxine (T4) to rule ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_249110588#1_446324151","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_06_249110588#2_446325846","rank":2,"relevance":2}]}}
